  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nPrecip and thunder chances...Broad warm air advection regime will\ncontinue to support a few showers or sprinkles into tonight. Rain\nwill end late. Maybe a touch of stratus/fog late tonight based on\nwealth of stratus upstream this morning. Shortwave trough currently\nover Pacific Northwest swings across northern Plains on Wednesday\ninto Wednesday night. Strongest forcing and primary surface low \nwith this tracks from Minnesota into Ontario Canada. Weak cold \nfront tied to the low crosses Wisconsin late Wednesday and Wednesday\nNight. Periods of showers (70-100%) expected for our area and with\nelevated instability over 500J/kg, a few non-severe embedded \nthunderstorms will occur. Bulk of rain occurs on Wednesday night.\nEffective shear and greater instability and even some surface \nbased instability from MO-IA into MN may support a higher severe \nrisk there, which lines up with Machine Learning output. Lingering\nshowers exit eastern Wisconsin Thursday morning, with dry weather\nreturning late Thursday into Thursday night. \n\nRainfall totals from Wednesday afternoon through Thursday morning\nstill look to remain on the light side, with probabilities of \nover 0.25\" 50-80% highest from central to north-central Wisconsin,\nand probabilities for over 0.50\" 30-50%, highest in far north- \ncentral Wisconsin. Any rain will help with the ongoing drought \nacross northern WI. \n\nUnsettled weather returns this weekend, including for Halloween\nactivities. Some scattered light showers around, but not a washout\nby any stretch, with NBM/LREF probabilities for more than 0.05\" \n30-60%, highest over central Wisconsin. Looking farther ahead, a \nlot uncertainty but some indications at least off and on of a \nstronger system that may impact Wisconsin for Election Day with\ngusty winds and rain.  \n\nTemperatures...Plan on a quick rebound in temperatures from this \nearly weeks chill. Warm sector living will boost highs into the \nupper 60s to lower 70s on Wednesday. Some record highs could be \nbroken, but if low clouds linger longer into the day that would \nput a crimp into that. Record highs for Wednesday (10/28) are 70 \nat Green Bay (1946), 71 at Wausau (1986) and 78 at Rhinelander  \n(1927). Slight cooling on Thursday behind the weak front, most \nnotable in the north. Highs elsewhere still reach the mid 60s. \nAverage highs for late October are in the low 50s. On into the \nweekend, no cool down is in sight. Highs will be in the upper 50s \nto lower 60s, depending on clouds and coverage of showers. \nTemperatures may cool into the 50s next week, especially if the \nstronger system ends up verifying over the region as the ECMWF has\nshown recently and is hinted at by LREF precip and wind gusts \nprobabilities.",
